---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';

import sunsetTurbines from '../assets/photos/sunset-turbines.jpg';
import barnSunset from '../assets/photos/barn-sunset.jpg';
import sunsetWindow from '../assets/photos/sunset-window.jpg';
import goldenClouds from '../assets/photos/golden-clouds.jpg';
import milkyWay from '../assets/photos/milky-way.jpg';
import snowGutter from '../assets/photos/snow-gutter.jpg';
import wildflowers from '../assets/photos/wildflowers-yellow.jpg';
import paintbrush from '../assets/photos/paintbrush.jpg';
import primrose from '../assets/photos/primrose.jpg';
import tracks from '../assets/photos/animal-tracks.jpg';
import campfire from '../assets/photos/campfire.jpg';

const sizes = '(max-width: 40rem) 100vw, (max-width: 72rem) 50vw, 22rem';
const widths = [420, 700, 1100];
---
<Base
  title="The Seasons — Windrose Farm"
  description="A year on 37 acres of Arizona high desert: spring wildflowers, monsoon skies, winter frost, and some of the darkest night skies in the country."
>
  <section class="wrap article-header">
    <p class="eyebrow">The Seasons</p>
    <h1>A year on the land</h1>
    <p class="prose" style="color: var(--ink-soft);">
      Before anything is planted, the first job is to pay attention. These are
      the things the land does on its own — the blooms it produces without
      being asked, the storms that cross it, the animals that walk through at
      night, and the sky it gets to keep because nobody out here has built
      anything bright enough to spoil it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" style="padding-top: 0;">
    <div class="season-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Spring</p>
      <h2>What grows here anyway</h2>
      <p class="prose">
        The soil survey says this ground holds barely an inch and a half of
        available water. Every spring it answers back. None of these were
        planted, watered, or tended by us — they simply live here, which is the
        most encouraging thing a difficult piece of land can tell you.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="gallery">
      <figure>
        <Image src={wildflowers} alt="A spray of yellow wildflowers blooming low against red desert soil" widths={widths} sizes={sizes} />
        <figcaption><b>Volunteers</b>Yellow blooms coming up through gravel, entirely unassisted.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="portrait">
        <Image src={paintbrush} alt="Scarlet Indian paintbrush flowering among dry grass and red soil" widths={widths} sizes={sizes} />
        <figcaption><b>Indian paintbrush</b>A plant that roots partly on its neighbours — cooperation as a survival strategy.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="portrait">
        <Image src={primrose} alt="White evening primrose blossoms open against rocky red ground" widths={widths} sizes={sizes} />
        <figcaption><b>Evening primrose</b>Opens at dusk, closes by mid-morning. You have to be here at the right hour.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="season-head" style="margin-top: 0;">
        <p class="eyebrow">Summer &amp; Monsoon</p>
        <h2>Weather worth watching</h2>
        <p class="prose">
          Roughly twelve inches of rain a year arrives mostly in a few dramatic
          weeks. Watching exactly where that water runs is not idle
          sightseeing — it is the survey that decides where every swale gets cut.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="gallery">
        <figure>
          <Image src={goldenClouds} alt="Low sun lighting a bank of storm clouds gold above the high desert horizon" widths={widths} sizes={sizes} />
          <figcaption><b>Storm light</b>The kind of sky that arrives before the monsoon does its work.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure>
          <Image src={sunsetTurbines} alt="Rows of wind turbines silhouetted along the horizon against a deep orange sunset" widths={widths} sizes={sizes} />
          <figcaption><b>The windrose, literally</b>The turbines on the horizon harvest the same prevailing wind the farm is named for.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure>
          <Image src={barnSunset} alt="The barn silhouetted against a vivid orange and pink sunset, its windows catching the last light" widths={widths} sizes={sizes} />
          <figcaption><b>Last light on the barn</b>Six o'clock, looking west across the developed area.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap">
    <div class="season-head" style="margin-top: 0;">
      <p class="eyebrow">Autumn &amp; Winter</p>
      <h2>The quiet half of the year</h2>
      <p class="prose">
        Cold winters are part of the deal at 5,700 feet — and they are also why
        the planting list looks the way it does. Jujube, apples and asparagus
        all want a real dormancy. The cold is not an obstacle to work around;
        it is a requirement several of these crops insist on.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="gallery">
      <figure class="portrait">
        <Image src={snowGutter} alt="A line of snow resting along the barn's gutter and roof edge under a clear sky" widths={widths} sizes={sizes} />
        <figcaption><b>Snow on the gutter</b>Winter moisture, arriving slowly enough that the ground can actually take it in.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <Image src={sunsetWindow} alt="A sunset reflected whole in a window set into the barn's red metal siding" widths={widths} sizes={sizes} />
        <figcaption><b>Borrowed sky</b>The whole sunset, reflected in a barn window.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="portrait">
        <Image src={tracks} alt="Animal paw prints pressed into soft dirt beside a boot for scale" widths={widths} sizes={sizes} />
        <figcaption><b>Night traffic</b>Tracks across the drive — a reminder whose land this was first.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="season-head" style="margin-top: 0;">
        <p class="eyebrow">After Dark</p>
        <h2>The sky we get to keep</h2>
        <p class="prose">
          There is no town close enough to wash out the stars. On a clear
          moonless night the Milky Way is plainly visible overhead — an
          unglamorous consequence of being a long way from everything, and one
          of the best things about the place.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="gallery">
        <figure class="portrait">
          <Image src={milkyWay} alt="The Milky Way arching across a dense field of stars in a completely dark sky" widths={widths} sizes={sizes} />
          <figcaption><b>Milky Way</b>Straight up, no telescope, no filter.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure>
          <Image src={campfire} alt="A fire burning in a steel fire pit at dusk beside a camper, under a cloud-streaked sky" widths={widths} sizes={sizes} />
          <figcaption><b>Why we come out</b>"A gathering place for family and friends" — this is what that looks like so far.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap">
    <p class="prose" style="color: var(--ink-soft);">
      More will be added as the years go by — the first blossom, the first
      harvest, the first hive. That is rather the point of a project measured
      in decades. Follow along in <a href="/journal/">the journal</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
